✅ Question 1: Why do we need Azure Data Factory (ADF)?
ADF doesn’t store data. Instead, it lets you:
Create data-driven workflows
Move and transform data between different stores
Monitor and manage those workflows using UI or code

✅ Question 2: What is Azure Data Factory?
ADF is a cloud-based data integration service used for:
Creating pipelines
Automating data movement and transformation
Running them on a scheduled basis (hourly, daily, weekly)

✅ Question 3: What is Integration Runtime (IR)?
Integration Runtime is the infrastructure ADF uses to:
Move data between environments
Execute data transformation
There are 3 types:
Azure IR – For cloud-based data movements
Self-hosted IR – Installed on on-prem or VM
Azure SSIS IR – For executing SSIS packages in ADF

✅ Question 4: Is there a limit on Integration Runtimes?
No hard limit on instances, but there is a VM core limit per subscription (especially relevant for SSIS).

✅ Question 5: Azure Data Lake vs. Azure Data Warehouse
| Feature             | Data Lake                        | Data Warehouse          |
| ------------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Data type           | Raw, unstructured                | Structured, filtered    |
| Processing language | One (e.g., U-SQL)                | SQL                     |
| Storage             | Scalable for logs, IoT, big data | Optimized for analytics |


